statement:
Two strings `s` and `t` are **isomorphic** if the characters of `s` can be replaced to obtain `t`, using one consistent replacement rule:

- every occurrence of a given character of `s` is replaced by the same character of `t`;
- two different characters of `s` may not be replaced by the same character of `t`;
- a character may be replaced by itself, and the order of the characters never changes.

Strings of different lengths can never be isomorphic. Characters are case-sensitive and may be any printable ASCII symbol other than a space, so letters, digits and punctuation are all possible.

Print `true` if `s` and `t` are isomorphic and `false` otherwise.

